--TEST--
A program whose one class of its own implements `Iterator<int>` still drives a generator through `Iterator<int>` with the generator's own `advance` and `current`, because the runtime makes cursors no class list of the program shows — `rule:iteration/two-interfaces`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The only class in the program that implements `Iterator<int>`.
class Countdown implements Iterator<int>
{
    private int $at = 3;

    public function advance(): bool
    {
        $this->at = $this->at - 1;
        return $this->at >= 0;
    }

    public function current(): int
    {
        return $this->at;
    }
}

class Pages
{
    public static function numbers(): Iterator<int>
    {
        yield 10;
        yield 20;
    }
}

// A generator, held in a variable of type `Iterator<int>`.
Iterator<int> $generator = Pages::numbers();
while ($generator->advance()) {
    echo $generator->current(), "\n";
}

// The class's own cursor, through the same type.
Iterator<int> $countdown = new Countdown();
while ($countdown->advance()) {
    echo $countdown->current(), "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
10
20
2
1
0
